nel happens entirely via lockless, circular FIFO ring buffers composed of descriptors (pointers/offsets into the UMEM, packet lengths, and flags), avoiding system calls on every packet:Fill Ring (User -> Kernel): User space populates this ring with addresses of empty UMEM frames that the kernel/NIC can use to store incoming packets.Rx Ring (Kernel -> User): The kernel places descriptors here to notify user space that a packet has arrived in a specific UMEM chunk.Tx Ring (User -> Kernel): User space places descriptors of packets ready to be transmitted over the wire.Completion Ring (Kernel -> User): The kernel notifies user space when transmission is complete, signaling that the corresponding UMEM frame can be reused.Standard UDP Socket vs. AF_XDP SocketDimensionStandard UDP (AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM)AF_XDP (AF_XDP, SOCK_RAW)Packet LevelOperates on L4 payloads (the raw DNS payload).Operates on raw L2 Ethernet frames.Header HandlingKernel automatically creates/strips Ethernet, IPv4/IPv6, and UDP headers.User space must manually craft and parse Ethernet, IP, and UDP headers and calculate checksums.Kernel Stack TraversalPasses through netfilter, iptables/nftables, routing, socket buffers (sk_buff), queuing disciplines (qdisc).Bypasses the entire kernel network stack; diverted directly at the NIC driver layer.Data CopyingTwo copies: NIC DMA to kernel sk_buff, then kernel buffer to user buffer via recvfrom().Zero-copy (if supported by NIC driver): NIC DMAs directly into user-allocated UMEM chunks.System Call OverheadSystem calls (sendto/recvfrom) invoked per packet (or batch via recvmmsg). High context-switch cost.Ring buffers are polled in user space; poll() or sendto() is only used to kick the kernel when rings fill/drain.End-to-End Workflow ComparisonCase 1: Resolving via Standard UDP SocketsNIC RX -> Driver -> sk_buff alloc -> Netfilter/IP/UDP Stack -> Socket Queue -> recvfrom() [Copy to User]
